 To find the minimum value of $a^2+6a-7$, we complete the square.

We add and subtract $\left(\frac{6}{2}\right)^2=9$ to get \begin{align*}
a^2+6a-7&=a^2+6a+9-9-7=
(a+3)^2-16
\end{align*} We know that $(a+3)^2$ is nonnegative, because $(a+3)^2\ge 0$.  Therefore, \begin{align*} (a+3)^2-16\ge -16
\end{align*}

We see that the minimum value of $(a+3)^2-16$ (or $a^2+6a-7$) is $-16$ and in this case $(a+3)^2=0$, which occurs when $a=-3$. This will give us the minimum value of $\boxed{-16}$.   For example, you can set $a=-6$ to get an $a^2+6a-7$ of $(-6)^2+6(-6)-7= -16$.
